Each module can represent a team’s area or business capability.
The Plugins.Core library provides shared base classes (for example, BasePlugin, TracingHelper, ServiceFactory).

2. shared core llibrary

public abstract class BasePlugin : IPlugin
{
    protected ITracingService Tracer { get; private set; }
    protected IOrganizationService Service { get; private set; }

    public void Execute(IServiceProvider serviceProvider)
    {
        Tracer = (ITracingService)serviceProvider.GetService(typeof(ITracingService));
        var context = (IPluginExecutionContext)serviceProvider.GetService(typeof(IPluginExecutionContext));
        var factory = (IOrganizationServiceFactory)serviceProvider.GetService(typeof(IOrganizationServiceFactory));
        Service = factory.CreateOrganizationService(context.UserId);
        ExecutePluginLogic(context);
    }

    protected abstract void ExecutePluginLogic(IPluginExecutionContext context);
}


2. Shared Core Library

All plugins reference a single Core project:

public abstract class BasePlugin : IPlugin
{
    protected ITracingService Tracer { get; private set; }
    protected IOrganizationService Service { get; private set; }

    public void Execute(IServiceProvider serviceProvider)
    {
        Tracer = (ITracingService)serviceProvider.GetService(typeof(ITracingService));
        var context = (IPluginExecutionContext)serviceProvider.GetService(typeof(IPluginExecutionContext));
        var factory = (IOrganizationServiceFactory)serviceProvider.GetService(typeof(IOrganizationServiceFactory));
        Service = factory.CreateOrganizationService(context.UserId);
        ExecutePluginLogic(context);
    }

    protected abstract void ExecutePluginLogic(IPluginExecutionContext context);
}


Then individual plugins inherit from it:

public class ContactUpdatePlugin : BasePlugin
{
    protected override void ExecutePluginLogic(IPluginExecutionContext context)
    {
        // custom logic here
    }
}

Each module can represent a team’s area or business capability.
The Plugins.Core library provides shared base classes (for example, BasePlugin, TracingHelper, ServiceFactory).




3. Unified Build and Deployment

Use a solution folder or NuGet package consolidation to make builds consistent:

Centralize common NuGet dependencies (Microsoft.CrmSdk.*, Newtonsoft.Json, etc.)

Use a single CI/CD pipeline (Azure DevOps, GitHub Actions, etc.)

Package all DLLs into one .zip or .nupkg for deployment using PAC CLI.

You can also:

Combine all plugin assemblies into one by using ILMerge or MSBuild tasks, or

Keep separate assemblies and deploy them all via script.


4. automated registration:
Option 1: pac plugin push

Registers all plugins from the DLL or directory:
pac plugin push --connection "ProdConn" --assembly bin/Release/net8.0/Plugins.Account.dll


Option 2: spkl

Add a spkl.json file:

{
  "plugins": [
    {
      "assemblypath": "bin/Release/net8.0/Plugins.Account.dll",
      "solution": "MySolution",
      "profile": "debug",
      "configuration": "debug"
    }
  ]
}

Then run:

spkl plugins


5. Recommended Patterns

Dependency Injection: Use a lightweight DI container (like SimpleInjector or Autofac) within plugin constructors.

Logging: Centralize all tracing to a shared class or table.

Configuration: Store plugin settings in EnvironmentVariableDefinition or Dataverse configuration tables.

Testing: Create a Plugins.Tests project with unit tests that mock the IPluginExecutionContext and IOrganizationService.

6. folder-based organization with namespace:

Plugins.Sales.Account.Create
Plugins.Sales.Account.Update
Plugins.Sales.Opportunity.Calculate
Plugins.Support.Ticket.Assign
Plugins.Support.Ticket.Close



